WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.240
Imagine you've grown a strawberry plant with the

00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:03.440
fruit you want.

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:05.680
You can make new plants from it, so

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:08.320
why start again with an uncertain result?

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:10.320
Copying sounds useful.

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:13.120
Then a new disease reaches the farm.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:15.440
Could those copies share a weakness as well

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:17.760
as the fruit characteristics you wanted?

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:19.600
To answer that, we need to follow what

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:21.200
a new plant inherits.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:25.360
First, a plant growing more leaves is still

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:26.720
the same individual.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:30.720
Reproduction makes another individual: an offspring.

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:33.680
Here, the small plant has its own roots

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:34.640
and leaves.

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:38.000
Once established, it can grow separately, and later

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:39.960
it can make offspring too.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:42.800
The original plant will not live for ever,

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:44.720
but another generation can follow it.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:47.920
The new plant also receives DNA from its

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:48.720
parent.

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:53.520
DNA carries inherited information, information passed from parent

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:57.200
to offspring, that helps shape the plant's characteristics.

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:00.720
For the population to continue, enough offspring must

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:02.880
survive and reproduce in turn.

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:06.180
The connecting stem is a runner.

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:08.340
A new plant grows from it without a

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:10.420
sperm cell and an egg cell joining.

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:13.140
We call that asexual reproduction.

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:16.020
As the new cells form, they copy the

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:17.380
parent's DNA.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:20.500
The daughter usually has almost the same inherited

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:23.300
information as its parent, so we call it

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:24.580
a clone.

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:27.220
That's why runners are useful to our grower:

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:30.420
they can keep a valued inherited combination instead

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:33.460
of mixing it with information from another plant.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:36.340
Similar DNA does not mean every plant grows

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:40.020
exactly alike, because growing conditions still matter.

00:01:41.234 --> 00:01:43.713
A strawberry plant can also make offspring through

00:01:43.794 --> 00:01:44.433
a flower.

00:01:44.994 --> 00:01:48.513
Consider a cross between two genetically different plants,

00:01:48.674 --> 00:01:50.753
meaning their inherited DNA differs.

00:01:51.394 --> 00:01:53.793
Inside a flower of one parent, a sperm

00:01:53.874 --> 00:01:56.033
cell from the other joins an egg cell.

00:01:56.594 --> 00:01:59.953
Sperm and egg are reproductive cells called gametes.

00:02:00.354 --> 00:02:02.193
Each contributes DNA.

00:02:02.514 --> 00:02:05.553
Reproduction in which gametes join is called sexual

00:02:05.634 --> 00:02:06.673
reproduction.

00:02:06.994 --> 00:02:09.713
The developing new plant is inside a seed,

00:02:09.794 --> 00:02:12.433
and that seed can grow into a seedling.

00:02:12.594 --> 00:02:15.313
So the runner and this cross both produce

00:02:15.394 --> 00:02:18.673
new plants, but the cross brings inherited information

00:02:18.754 --> 00:02:20.353
from the two parents together.

00:02:21.567 --> 00:02:23.966
How can the same two parents produce different

00:02:24.047 --> 00:02:24.966
seedlings?

00:02:25.247 --> 00:02:28.126
Different sperm cells can carry different selections of

00:02:28.207 --> 00:02:30.446
one parent's inherited information.

00:02:30.767 --> 00:02:33.006
Egg cells can differ in the same way.

00:02:33.327 --> 00:02:36.286
When different pairs join, the seedlings can receive

00:02:36.367 --> 00:02:38.846
different combinations of DNA.

00:02:38.927 --> 00:02:43.126
Those differences in inherited information are genetic variation.

00:02:43.487 --> 00:02:46.526
Here is the contrast we need: runner offspring

00:02:46.687 --> 00:02:49.966
usually retain the parent's combination, while offspring from

00:02:50.047 --> 00:02:53.166
this cross can inherit different combinations.

00:02:53.287 --> 00:02:56.046
That gives the grower different plants to compare.

00:02:56.127 --> 00:02:57.806
It does not tell us which one will

00:02:58.007 --> 00:02:58.846
perform best.

00:03:00.067 --> 00:03:02.426
Now return to the disease at the farm.

00:03:02.707 --> 00:03:05.106
The grower copied a plant because its inherited

00:03:05.187 --> 00:03:06.706
characteristics were useful.

00:03:07.347 --> 00:03:09.506
But if that plant also has an inherited

00:03:09.667 --> 00:03:12.466
weakness against the new disease, its clones may

00:03:12.627 --> 00:03:13.106
share it.

00:03:13.747 --> 00:03:16.386
That is a shared risk, not proof that

00:03:16.467 --> 00:03:18.586
every plant will become infected.

00:03:19.027 --> 00:03:23.026
Seedlings with different inherited combinations may respond differently,

00:03:23.107 --> 00:03:25.266
if the parents carry differences that matter for

00:03:25.347 --> 00:03:26.866
this disease.

00:03:27.107 --> 00:03:30.626
Copying helps keep a combination we already value.

00:03:30.787 --> 00:03:34.066
Sexual reproduction can offer different combinations to compare

00:03:34.147 --> 00:03:36.146
when conditions change.

00:03:36.227 --> 00:03:39.266
For this grower, a useful reminder is: copy

00:03:39.347 --> 00:03:41.906
to keep, cross to compare.

00:03:41.987 --> 00:03:44.466
The choice still depends on the conditions and

00:03:44.507 --> 00:03:45.346
the purpose.

00:03:46.567 --> 00:03:49.126
A nursery wants to find strawberry plants with

00:03:49.287 --> 00:03:51.846
different inherited responses to drier soil.

00:03:52.407 --> 00:03:54.406
It can start with runner offspring from one

00:03:54.487 --> 00:03:58.086
parent, or seedlings from crosses between genetically different

00:03:58.167 --> 00:03:58.726
parents.

00:03:59.367 --> 00:04:01.206
Neither group has been tested in the drier

00:04:01.287 --> 00:04:01.766
soil.

00:04:02.327 --> 00:04:04.486
Which group would you choose for this search?

00:04:04.887 --> 00:04:08.006
Explain your choice, then name one result you

00:04:08.207 --> 00:04:09.486
cannot predict yet.

00:04:09.767 --> 00:04:11.206
Pause here to decide.

00:04:23.634 --> 00:04:26.113
The crossed seedlings are the better starting group

00:04:26.154 --> 00:04:29.233
for a search for different inherited responses.

00:04:29.314 --> 00:04:32.593
They receive DNA through sexual reproduction from genetically

00:04:32.674 --> 00:04:37.153
different parents, so they can inherit different combinations.

00:04:37.314 --> 00:04:40.113
Runner offspring usually copy a much narrower set

00:04:40.194 --> 00:04:42.673
of inherited information from one parent.

00:04:42.994 --> 00:04:45.473
But we still cannot predict which seedling will

00:04:45.554 --> 00:04:47.353
grow well in the dry soil.

00:04:47.634 --> 00:04:50.033
The parents may not carry useful differences for

00:04:50.114 --> 00:04:52.993
that condition, and a particular seedling may not

00:04:53.074 --> 00:04:54.753
receive a useful combination.

00:04:55.314 --> 00:04:57.233
We would need to compare how the plants

00:04:57.394 --> 00:04:58.593
actually grow there.

00:04:59.234 --> 00:05:02.833
More variation gives us possibilities to investigate, not

00:05:02.914 --> 00:05:03.953
a promised winner.

00:05:05.167 --> 00:05:09.406
Reproduction provides new individuals and passes inherited information

00:05:09.487 --> 00:05:11.006
to another generation.

00:05:11.327 --> 00:05:15.166
Copying can keep a useful combination; sexual reproduction

00:05:15.247 --> 00:05:17.646
can produce different inherited combinations.

00:05:18.207 --> 00:05:21.486
Neither route guarantees that offspring will survive.

00:05:21.967 --> 00:05:24.206
Next, we'll look at how different animals bring

00:05:24.287 --> 00:05:27.006
sperm and egg together and support the development

00:05:27.087 --> 00:05:28.166
of their offspring.

00:05:28.447 --> 00:05:30.846
The detailed plant mechanisms follow in their own

00:05:30.927 --> 00:05:31.566
lessons.

00:05:32.767 --> 00:05:34.686
If you'd like a short set of notes,

00:05:34.807 --> 00:05:37.566
copy these now, or pause for more time.
